::GEO_U01_L08_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L08_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best explains why a copied segment is accurate? {
=A. The compass opening from the original segment was transferred without changing it#Correct. A fixed compass opening preserves the original segment length.
~B. The copied segment looks close to the original length#Appearance alone does not prove the segment lengths match.
~C. The copied segment was labeled with new letters#Labels name the segment but do not prove equal length.
~D. The copied segment was drawn darker than the original#Line darkness is not construction evidence.
}

::GEO_U01_L08_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L08_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>If line &ell; is the perpendicular bisector of segment AB at point M, which statement must be true? {
~A. AM > MB#A bisector creates equal segment parts.
=B. AM = MB and line &ell; forms right angles with AB#Correct. A perpendicular bisector bisects the segment and is perpendicular to it.
~C. AB = M#A segment length is not equal to a point.
~D. Line &ell; copies &angle;ABC#A perpendicular bisector is not an angle-copy construction.
}

::GEO_U01_L08_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L08_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the contrapositive of If p, then q? {
~A. If q, then p#This is the converse, not the contrapositive.
~B. If not p, then not q#This is the inverse, not the contrapositive.
=C. If not q, then not p#Correct. The contrapositive switches and negates both parts.
~D. If p, then not q#This negates only the conclusion.
}

::GEO_U01_L08_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L08_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>If &angle;A and &angle;B form a linear pair, what is m&angle;A + m&angle;B? {
~A. 90&deg;#A linear pair forms a straight angle, not necessarily a right angle.
~B. 100&deg;#Linear-pair angles are supplementary, so this sum is too small.
=C. 180&deg;#Correct. Angles that form a linear pair are supplementary.
~D. 360&deg;#A linear pair forms a straight angle, not a full rotation.
}

::GEO_U01_L08_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L08_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which counterexample disproves the claim All rectangles are squares? {
~A. A square with side length 5#A square is a rectangle and does not disprove the claim.
=B. A rectangle with side lengths 4 and 7#Correct. It is a rectangle but not a square.
~C. A triangle with three equal sides#A triangle is not a rectangle.
~D. A circle with radius 4#A circle is not a rectangle.
}
